# Gather context

Phase 0 of a build, extracted so the decisions get made once and written down.
The output is a dossier; the build session reads it and skips its own setup.

## Communication discipline

Report what was measured, not what is planned. One line per finding, with the
command or file that produced it. No status monologue, no restating the request.

## Cardinal rules

1. Verify every remembered path, flag, command and version against the live tree
   before writing it into the dossier. The source wins.
2. Never commit, push, branch-force or deploy. Creating a working branch is the
   only git write this pass may make, and only when the owner's rules allow it.
3. Do not start building. This pass ends at the dossier.

## Step 1 — Card intake

When the input is a card id, read the card first: objective, source document,
files, acceptance criteria, out-of-scope, dependencies. Read the linked
requirements or design document before forming any opinion. If the card and the
tree disagree, record the disagreement in the dossier rather than silently
picking one.

## Step 2 — Context audit

Decide what this build actually needs loaded, and what it does not.

**Audit:** the surfaces the card names; their immediate callers; the tests that
cover them; the configuration that gates them.

**Propose:** which parts are read once and summarised, which are delegated to a
subagent, and which stay in the main thread because the build edits them
directly.

**Drop:** everything the card puts out of scope, plus any large generated
artifact. Name what was dropped, so the build session knows it was a decision
and not an oversight.

Pause and confirm the plan with the owner when the audit proposes dropping
something the card names, or when the build spans more than one repository.
Skip the pause for a single-file change with an obvious blast radius.

## Step 3 — The dossier

Write one document with these sections: intake (card, source, objective);
targets (repository, branch, surfaces); pre-flight (toolchain versions, whether
the tree is clean, whether the suite is green before any change); triage (the
test layers selected and why); delegation plan; and the handoff — the first
three actions the build session should take, in order.

Give it `answers:` frontmatter. A dossier nobody can find is a dossier nobody
reuses.

## Step 4 — Setup

Detect whether a previous run of this pass already produced a dossier; if so,
reconcile with it rather than duplicating it. Detect the target repository from
the card's file paths, not from memory. Create the working branch only if the
owner's rules permit it, and record the branch name in the dossier.

## Step 5 — Pre-flight

Run the build and the existing test suite BEFORE changing anything, and record
the result. A suite that was already red is a fact the build session must know;
discovering it mid-build costs a session.

## Step 6 — Triage the test layers

Pick the layers this change actually needs — unit, integration, end-to-end,
manual walk — using the blast radius, not habit. A pure-presentation change does
not need a database fixture; a migration does. Record the choice and the reason,
and confirm it with the owner when a layer is deliberately skipped.

## Step 7 — Hand off

Close with: where the dossier is, the branch, the pre-flight result, the
selected layers, and the first action for the build session. Then stop.

## Anti-patterns

- Loading the whole repository "for context".
- Recording a version or a path from memory.
- Creating a branch nobody asked for.
- Continuing into the build because the setup went well.
